\f0\b\fs44 \cf2 Diet and Macronutrient Calculator Specification
\f1\b0\fs28 \
\
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\sl324\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34 \cf2 Overview
\f1\b0\fs28 \
\
This diet and macronutrient calculator is a comprehensive tool for personalized nutrition planning. It uses evidence-based formulas and guidelines to estimate an individual\'92s Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) and optimal macronutrient targets. Users input personal data (age, gender, body composition, etc.) and goals, and the calculator outputs daily calorie needs, macronutrient breakdowns (in both grams and % of calories), and optional micronutrient recommendations. The calculator accommodates various diets (e.g. vegan, keto, paleo) and goals (weight loss, maintenance, muscle gain), with advanced settings for manual overrides. It is adaptable for the general population as well as specific groups like athletes and older adults. Key reference equations (Mifflin-St Jeor, Cunningham, etc.) and dietary guidelines are incorporated   to ensure accuracy and credibility.\
\

\f0\b\fs34 User Inputs
\f1\b0\fs28 \
\
To generate personalized results, the calculator accepts the following inputs:\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Age & Gender:
\f1\b0  Used in metabolic equations (e.g. Mifflin-St Jeor) since basal calorie needs vary by age and sex . Age also factors into life-stage recommendations (older adults may need more protein , and women of childbearing age have unique needs).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Body Weight & Height:
\f1\b0  Used for calculating resting metabolic rate if not directly provided. Height and weight (along with age and gender) feed into formulas like Mifflin-St Jeor . Weight is also used to scale macronutrient needs (e.g. protein per kg body weight).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Body Fat Percentage:
\f1\b0  Allows use of body composition in calculations instead of BMI. With body fat %, the calculator can compute 
\f2\b Lean Body Mass (LBM)
\f1\b0  and use formulas like Katch-McArdle or Cunningham for RMR . For example, LBM = Weight \'d7 (1 \uc0\u8722  bodyFat%) . This yields a more personalized metabolic rate by accounting for muscle mass.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Resting Metabolic Rate (RMR):
\f1\b0  Optionally, a user can input a measured RMR (from indirect calorimetry or fitness trackers). If provided, this value overrides calculated RMR. If not provided, the calculator will estimate RMR using a chosen equation. 
\f2\b Basal metabolic rate (BMR)
\f1\b0  is often used interchangeably with RMR for calculation purposes; RMR is slightly higher but in practice formulas like Mifflin-St Jeor predict an RMR/BMR value.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Physical Activity Level (PAL):
\f1\b0  A factor or category describing daily activity and exercise level. The user can select an activity multiplier or input a custom value. For example: Sedentary (PAL \uc0\u8776 1.2), Lightly Active (\u8776 1.375), Moderately Active (\u8776 1.55), Very Active (\u8776 1.725), Extra Active (\u8776 1.9) . These multipliers represent how much above RMR one\'92s daily calorie burn is, based on work, exercise frequency, etc. (See 
\f2\b TDEE Calculation
\f1\b0  below).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Thermic Effect of Food (TEF):
\f1\b0  The user may allow the calculator to apply a default value (around 10% of caloric intake ) or manually adjust this if they have a specific diet composition. TEF (diet-induced thermogenesis) is the energy expended to digest and process food. High-protein diets raise TEF (protein\'92s TEF ~20\'9630% of its calories, vs ~5\'9610% for carbs and ~0\'963% for fats ). Advanced users can tweak the TEF percentage if, for example, they follow a very high-protein regimen or have data on their personal thermogenesis.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Dietary Preferences:
\f1\b0  Users can specify if they follow a particular diet pattern:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Vegan/Vegetarian:
\f1\i0  The calculator will account for the absence of animal products. While macro targets can remain similar, the tool may suggest higher protein intake from plant sources (due to lower protein bioavailability) and flag crucial micronutrients (e.g. vitamin B\uc0\u8321 \u8322 , iron, calcium) for vegans . No animal-derived foods will be suggested.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Ketogenic (Keto):
\f1\i0  A very low-carb, high-fat diet. The calculator can switch to a keto macro ratio (typically ~5\'9610% of calories from carbs, ~20% protein, ~70\'9675% fat ). It may also disable the TEF override (since a keto diet\'92s high fat content yields a slightly lower TEF).\
	\'95	
\f3\i Low-Carb:
\f1\i0  A less extreme reduction in carbs than keto. Often defined as <130 g or <26% of calories from carbs . The user can choose a low-carb setting, and the calculator will allocate a higher percentage of calories to protein and fat accordingly.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Paleo:
\f1\i0  Focuses on whole foods (lean meats, veggies, fruit, nuts; excluding grains, legumes, dairy, processed foods). Paleo isn\'92t a fixed macro ratio, but it tends to be high in protein, moderate in fat (emphasizing unsaturated fats), and moderate to lower in carbs . The calculator in paleo mode can default to a balanced macro spread (for example, roughly 30% protein, 30% carb, 40% fat) and highlight high-fiber, unprocessed food choices.\
	\'95	
\f3\i (Other diets)
\f1\i0  can be accommodated similarly. For instance, a \'93High-Protein\'94 mode might ensure protein is at the upper recommended range; a \'93Mediterranean\'94 mode might emphasize unsaturated fats and moderate protein. The framework is flexible to include additional dietary patterns.\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Allergies/Restrictions:
\f1\b0  Users can list any food allergies or intolerances (e.g. nut allergy, gluten-free, lactose intolerance). While this doesn\'92t change macro needs, it guides the qualitative recommendations (e.g. suggesting alternative fat sources if nuts are avoided, or calcium sources beyond dairy if lactose intolerant). The calculator\'92s output can include notes or options that respect these restrictions.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Goal:
\f1\b0  The user\'92s goal is critical for calorie and macro adjustments:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Weight Loss:
\f1\i0  The calculator will propose a caloric intake below maintenance (TDEE). By default, a moderate deficit is used (commonly 500 kcal/day deficit for ~1 lb/week loss , or ~15\'9620% below TDEE). The user can adjust the aggressiveness (e.g. 250 kcal deficit for slower loss, or 750+ for faster loss, with appropriate caution). Macro distribution might emphasize protein to preserve lean mass during caloric restriction .\
	\'95	
\f3\i Weight Maintenance:
\f1\i0  Calorie target equals the TDEE (no deficit or surplus). Macros are set within normal healthy ranges unless customized by the user. This is the default mode.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Muscle Gain (Weight Gain):
\f1\i0  The calculator will add a caloric surplus above TDEE. A conservative surplus (typically +300\'96500 kcal/day) is recommended to promote muscle growth while minimizing fat gain . Users can specify the surplus size. Macro recommendations in a gain phase usually ensure ample protein for muscle synthesis and sufficient carbs to fuel training.\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Advanced/Other Inputs:
\f1\b0  Advanced users may input additional parameters for finer control. Examples include:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	Specific 
\f2\b exercise calories
\f1\b0  or training routines (to precisely calculate activity energy instead of using a generic PAL factor).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Stress or injury factors
\f1\b0  (for clinical use, a stress/injury factor sometimes adjusts calorie needs, but for a general tool this might be beyond scope).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Life-stage modifiers:
\f1\b0  e.g. if \'93Pregnant\'94 or \'93Breastfeeding\'94 is selected, the calculator could add evidence-based calorie needs (e.g. +340 kcal in 2nd trimester, +450 kcal in 3rd trimester ; +500 kcal for lactation).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Micronutrient targets:
\f1\b0  if the user wants to customize specific micronutrient goals (like higher iron intake for anemia, etc.), they could input or select these preferences for the output to consider.\
\
All inputs have sensible defaults or typical choices, but the user can override values (especially in an \'93advanced mode\'94). For example, if a user knows their exact measured RMR or prefers a specific macro ratio, they can manually input those values.\
\
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\sl324\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34 \cf2 Calculation of TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure)
\f1\b0\fs28 \
\
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\sl324\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf2 TDEE
\f1\b0  represents the total calories a person burns in a day, combining basal metabolism, activity, and digestion. The calculator computes TDEE through the following steps:\
\pard\tqr\tx260\tx420\li420\fi-420\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf2 	1.	
\f2\b Calculate Resting Metabolic Rate (RMR):
\f1\b0  This is the energy expended at rest. The calculator will use one of the established equations, unless an RMR is provided by the user.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Mifflin-St Jeor Equation:
\f1\i0  (Default) Widely regarded as one of the most accurate for general population . It requires weight, height, age, and gender:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Male:
\f1\b0  RMR = 10 \'d7 weight (kg) + 6.25 \'d7 height (cm) \'96 5 \'d7 age (y) + 5\
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\sl324\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf2 Female:
\f1\b0  RMR = 10 \'d7 weight (kg) + 6.25 \'d7 height (cm) \'96 5 \'d7 age (y) \'96 161\
(This yields RMR in kcal/day .)\
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\sl324\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i \cf2 Rationale:
\f1\i0  Mifflin-St Jeor was found to predict RMR within 10% of measured values in more individuals than other formulas . It has largely replaced the older Harris-Benedict formula from 1919.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Katch-McArdle Formula:
\f1\i0  Uses lean body mass (LBM) if body fat % is known .\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b BMR
\f1\b0  = 370 + 21.6 \'d7 LBM (kg) . (This formula gives basal calories based on muscle mass.)\
	\'95	LBM is calculated from weight and body fat %. For example, a 80 kg person with 20% fat has LBM = 80 \'d7 (1 \'96 0.20) = 64 kg.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Cunningham Equation:
\f1\i0  Another lean-mass-based formula, often used for athletes or very muscular individuals:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b RMR
\f1\b0  = 500 + 22 \'d7 LBM (kg) . (In an example, an athlete with 58 kg LBM would have RMR \uc0\u8776  500 + 22\'d758 \u8776  1776 kcal  .)\
	\'95	Cunningham is essentially an alternate form of Katch-McArdle with a different constant; it tends to give slightly higher values, better reflecting needs of those with above-average muscle mass.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Other Equations:
\f1\i0  The calculator can offer Harris-Benedict as an option for comparison or for completeness. Harris-Benedict (revised 1984) has formulas similar in structure to Mifflin (different coefficients) but tends to overestimate RMR in modern populations . The user could also select the 
\f2\b Schofield
\f1\b0  or 
\f2\b WHO
\f1\b0  equations if needed, or any specialized formula (e.g. 
\f2\b Owen
\f1\b0  formula) if provided, but these are generally not necessary for most users.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Direct RMR input:
\f1\i0  If the user enters their own RMR (from a metabolic test or fitness tracker), the calculator will skip the above formula calculations and use the given value. The interface might allow toggling \'93Use custom RMR\'94 to input this. This caters to advanced users or clinical scenarios (e.g. measured via indirect calorimetry).\
\pard\tqr\tx260\tx420\li420\fi-420\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf2 	2.	
\f2\b Incorporate Physical Activity:
\f1\b0  To go from RMR to 
\f3\i maintenance calories
\f1\i0 , the calculator multiplies by a 
\f2\b Physical Activity Level (PAL)
\f1\b0  factor. This accounts for exercise, job activity, and general lifestyle movement.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	Users can choose standard activity categories, which map to multipliers as follows :\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Sedentary (PAL ~1.2):
\f1\b0  Little to no exercise, desk job lifestyle.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Lightly Active (PAL ~1.375):
\f1\b0  Light exercise 1\'963 days/week or a job with mild activity.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Moderately Active (PAL ~1.55):
\f1\b0  Moderate exercise 3\'965 days/week (e.g. regular workouts) or a somewhat active job (e.g. teacher, retail).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Very Active (PAL ~1.725):
\f1\b0  Hard exercise 6\'967 days/week or physical job (e.g. construction worker, full-time athlete).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Extra Active (PAL ~1.9):
\f1\b0  Very intense daily training or extremely physical job, possibly twice-a-day workouts.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	The calculator multiplies RMR by the PAL factor to estimate 
\f2\b Calories from Activity + RMR
\f1\b0 . For example, if RMR = 1600 kcal and PAL = 1.55 (moderate), then unadjusted TDEE = 1600 \'d7 1.55 \uc0\u8776  2480 kcal.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Custom Activity Input:
\f1\b0  In advanced mode, users might input exercise in terms of calories or MET-hours. In that case, the calculator could do: TDEE = RMR + Exercise Calories + Non-exercise activity (NEAT) + (then TEF). For simplicity, using PAL is an aggregate approach. If a user is, say, an athlete who knows they burn ~500 kcal/day through training, they might prefer to input that directly and use a base PAL for daily living (like 1.2) for more precision.\
\pard\tqr\tx260\tx420\li420\fi-420\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf2 	3.	
\f2\b Thermic Effect of Food (TEF) Addition:
\f1\b0  By default, PAL multipliers were empirically derived and usually 
\f3\i already account
\f1\i0  for an average TEF (~10%). However, to be explicit and adjustable, the calculator can calculate TEF separately:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	A common estimate is 
\f2\b 10% of total caloric intake
\f1\b0  . If using this method, the calculator might take the above \'93unadjusted TDEE\'94 and divide by 0.9 (or multiply by 1.1) to add 10% for TEF. For example, 2480 kcal with TEF becomes ~2755 kcal/day.\
	\'95	Alternatively, the calculator can first compute \'93TDEE without TEF\'94 = RMR \'d7 PAL, then compute TEF = 0.1 \'d7 (RMR \'d7 PAL) and add it, yielding the same result.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Diet Composition Influence:
\f1\b0  As an advanced nuance, TEF can be adjusted based on the chosen macro split:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	Protein has a high thermic effect (20\'9630% of protein calories burned in processing) while carbs are ~5\'9610% and fats ~0\'963% . If a user chooses a 
\f2\b high-protein diet
\f1\b0 , the calculator might bump TEF slightly above 10%. For a 
\f2\b high-fat ketogenic diet
\f1\b0 , TEF might be a bit lower. This could be an automatic adjustment or left for the user to tweak.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	The default assumption (10%) works for most mixed diets . The interface might label this as \'93Thermic Effect of Food (TEF): assumed 10% (you can adjust if your diet is very high in protein or very low in carbs).\'94\
\pard\tqr\tx260\tx420\li420\fi-420\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf2 	4.	
\f2\b Total Daily Energy Expenditure:
\f1\b0  After accounting for RMR, activity, and TEF, the result is the maintenance calorie requirement:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b TDEE = RMR \'d7 PAL + TEF
\f1\b0  (if TEF added on) \'96 or effectively 
\f3\i RMR \'d7 PAL \'d7 1.1
\f1\i0  (if 10% used). This is the calorie level to maintain current weight given the inputs.\
	\'95	The calculator will display TDEE as a key output. It\'92s often useful to show a breakdown of TDEE components for transparency. For example: 
\f3\i \'93Estimated RMR: 1600 kcal; Activity: +880 kcal (PAL 1.55); TEF: +250 kcal (10% of intake); 
\f5\b TDEE: ~2730 kcal/day
\f3\b0 .\'94
\f1\i0  This educates users on where the calories are going (some tools even chart this).\
	\'95	If the user provided a custom RMR, the output will note that (e.g. \'93Using user-provided RMR of X kcal\'94).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Special adjustments:
\f1\b0  If relevant inputs like pregnancy were given, the additional calories (e.g. \'93+340 kcal for 2nd trimester\'94) would be added after calculating the base TDEE. Similarly for goals, as described next.\
\pard\tqr\tx260\tx420\li420\fi-420\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf2 	5.	
\f2\b Adjust for Goal (Caloric Surplus or Deficit):
\f1\b0  The TDEE above is the maintenance level. For weight goals:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	In 
\f2\b weight loss mode
\f1\b0 , the calculator subtracts a user-selected calorie amount or percentage. Default might be 20% below TDEE or 500 kcal less (since a 500 kcal/day deficit is roughly 0.45 kg or 1 lb weight loss per week ). The output will explicitly state the target intake and the assumed weekly weight loss. For example: 
\f3\i \'93To lose ~0.5 kg per week, your daily target is 
\f5\b 2200 kcal
\f3\b0  (500 kcal below your maintenance of 2700 kcal).\'94
\f1\i0  It may also caution not to go below a certain minimum (to ensure nutritional needs are met).\
	\'95	In 
\f2\b muscle gain mode
\f1\b0 , the calculator adds calories. A conservative surplus around 300 kcal is suggested for lean gains , but the user can set this (some might choose +500 kcal for faster gains, accepting more fat gain). The output might say: 
\f3\i \'93Bulking goal: 
\f5\b 3200 kcal
\f3\b0  per day (approx. +400 kcal over maintenance, aiming for +0.3\'960.5% body weight per week).\'94
\f1\i0  The reference to expected weight gain rate (e.g. ~0.25\'960.5% of body weight weekly) helps manage expectations .\
	\'95	
\f2\b Maintenance goal
\f1\b0  simply keeps calories at TDEE. The tool might still allow minor tweaks if the user wants (e.g. to slowly recomp or adjust weight by very small amounts).\
	\'95	The calculator ensures that after applying a deficit or surplus, it recalculates the 
\f2\b new
\f1\b0  TEF if doing it explicitly. (Because consuming less or more changes the TEF slightly in absolute terms, though this fine point can usually be ignored for simplicity.)\
	\'95	The result of this step is the 
\f2\b daily calorie target
\f1\b0  tailored to the goal.\
\
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\sl324\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34 \cf2 Macronutrient Breakdown
\f1\b0\fs28 \
\
Once daily calories are determined (maintenance or goal-adjusted), the calculator computes a recommended macronutrient distribution. Macronutrients \'96 carbohydrates, protein, and fats \'96 are the sources of calories and will be customized in grams and as percentages of total calories.\
\
Key considerations and default recommendations for macros:\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b General Balance and Ranges:
\f1\b0  For healthy adults, standard reference ranges for macros (Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Ranges, AMDR) are:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Carbohydrates:
\f1\b0  45\'9665% of total calories\
	\'95	
\f2\b Fats:
\f1\b0  20\'9635% of total calories\
	\'95	
\f2\b Protein:
\f1\b0  10\'9635% of total calories .\
These ranges ensure sufficient intake and chronic disease risk reduction while allowing flexibility. The calculator will keep suggested macros within these bounds unless a specific diet (keto, etc.) is chosen that intentionally goes outside (with appropriate notes/warnings).\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Protein:
\f1\b0  The calculator prioritizes protein, as it\'92s crucial for body function and particularly important for muscle maintenance/gain and satiety during weight loss.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Baseline:
\f1\b0  The RDA minimum is ~0.8 g per kg body weight (\uc0\u8776 0.36 g/lb) for sedentary adults . However, many individuals benefit from higher protein:\
	\'95	
\f2\b Active Individuals/Athletes:
\f1\b0  Suggested intake 
\f2\b 1.2\'962.0 g/kg
\f1\b0  body weight . Research and sports nutrition guidelines indicate intakes in this range improve muscle repair and performance adaptations, and the upper end (~2.0 g/kg) is useful during intense training or calorie deficits to preserve muscle .\
	\'95	
\f2\b Weight Loss:
\f1\b0  The calculator may set protein toward the higher end (e.g. 1.5\'962.2 g/kg) when a deficit is chosen, to mitigate muscle loss. For example, a 70 kg person aiming to lose fat might be given ~130 g protein (\uc0\u8776 1.85 g/kg).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Muscle Gain:
\f1\b0  Protein will also be high (though beyond ~2.2 g/kg there\'92s likely no further benefit). Typically 1.6\'962.0 g/kg is sufficient even for bodybuilding goals, as excess beyond that is not significantly used for muscle-building . The calculator might cap suggestions around 2 g/kg by default, but allow user override for personal preference.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Older Adults:
\f1\b0  If age > ~60, the tool can recommend protein at least ~1.0\'961.2 g/kg , because seniors need more protein to preserve muscle mass and function (an elderly user of 70 kg might get ~70\'9684 g protein suggested instead of the 56 g that 0.8 g/kg would give). This aligns with the PROT-AGE study group recommendations for older populations .\
	\'95	
\f2\b Diet Preferences:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Vegan:
\f1\i0  The protein target might be set slightly higher or flagged to ensure adequacy, because plant proteins can be less bioavailable. The output will encourage a variety of protein sources (legumes, tofu, seitan, etc.) and likely mention supplementing 
\f2\b vitamin B\uc0\u8321 \u8322 
\f1\b0   and possibly 
\f2\b omega-3
\f1\b0  (flax/chia or algal oil) for vegans.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Keto/Paleo:
\f1\i0  Usually inherently higher in protein. The calculator will still ensure it\'92s within safe limits. For keto, protein is moderate (too high protein can kick one out of ketosis), typically ~20% of calories  \'96 the tool might set ~1.5 g/kg and then adjust carb to the very low threshold.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Output:
\f1\b0  Protein will be reported in grams per day and as a percentage of calories. For instance, the output might say: 
\f3\i \'93Protein: 130 g/day (26% of calories)\'94
\f1\i0 . It may also break that into per-meal suggestions or mention equivalents (especially for educational feedback, e.g. \'93~130 g is roughly what you\'92d get from 5 servings of lean protein like chicken breast, fish, or tofu throughout the day.\'94).\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Carbohydrates:
\f1\b0  Carbs are the body\'92s main fuel, especially for brain and athletic performance.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Baseline Range:
\f1\b0  The calculator\'92s default for a balanced diet might assign carbs ~45\'9655% of calories (unless otherwise specified by the user). For example, on a 2500 kcal maintenance diet, 50% carbs = 1250 kcal, which is ~312 g of carbs (since 1 g carb = 4 kcal).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Athletic Needs:
\f1\b0  Endurance athletes or those training frequently at high intensity often need higher carbs to replenish glycogen. The tool can accommodate this by either allowing a higher percent or by letting the user specify grams per kg. For example, an endurance athlete might target 
\f2\b 6\'9610 g/kg
\f1\b0  carbs on heavy training days . If the user flags themselves as an athlete or selects a high activity level, the calculator could suggest a higher carb percentage (e.g. 55\'9660% of calories, or even more on specific days if periodizing carbs).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Low-Carb/Keto:
\f1\b0  If the user chose a low-carb or ketogenic preference, the calculator will drastically reduce carbs:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Keto:
\f1\i0  Typically <50 g per day, often ~5\'9610% of calories . The output will highlight that this is extremely low (e.g. \'93Carbs: 30 g/day (8% of calories) \'96 
\f2\b Ketogenic level
\f1\b0 \'94) and may note the need for sufficient fiber and micronutrients from non-starchy vegetables. It might also warn that this is below the AMDR for carbs , but is intentional for ketosis.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Low-Carb (non-keto):
\f1\i0  The user can define what that means for them. A common threshold is <130 g/day . The calculator might default to, say, 25% calories from carbs (which on a 2000 kcal diet is 125 g, meeting that definition). It should ensure protein and fat are increased to compensate calorie-wise.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Fiber:
\f1\b0  The calculator can optionally include a fiber recommendation (e.g. \'93aim for 25\'9638 g of fiber per day\'94 as per general dietary guidelines). This isn\'92t a macro that contributes calories (fiber is usually counted within carbs but not fully absorbed), but it\'92s nutritionally relevant. If the user\'92s diet type might risk low fiber (like keto), the tool will definitely remind them to include fibrous low-carb veggies or supplements for gut health.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Output:
\f1\b0  Carbs are shown in grams and percent. For example: 
\f3\i \'93Carbohydrates: 300 g/day (50% of calories)\'94
\f1\i0 . If relevant, an explanation might follow, e.g. \'93Emphasize complex carbs (whole grains, fruits, vegetables) for sustained energy and nutrients. For low-carb diets, focus on non-starchy vegetables and allowable fruits.\'94\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Fats:
\f1\b0  Dietary fat is essential for hormone production, nutrient absorption, and satiety.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Baseline Range:
\f1\b0  Typically set to ~20\'9635% of calories . For instance, a balanced plan might use ~30% of calories from fat. On 2500 kcal, 30% = 750 kcal, which is ~83 g of fat (9 kcal per gram of fat).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Lower Limit:
\f1\b0  The calculator should enforce a minimum fat intake (e.g. no less than ~15\'9620% of calories, or ~0.5 g/kg body weight) because extremely low fat can impair absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and hormone levels. If a user tries to set fat too low, the tool could provide a warning or adjust it up.\
	\'95	
\f2\b High-Fat Diets:
\f1\b0  For keto or high-fat preference, fat will be set high. In keto mode, fat might be ~70% or more of calories . The output will likely stress choosing healthy fats (unsaturated fats from fish, olive oil, nuts, avocado, etc.) to align with health recommendations, and limiting trans fats or excessive saturated fats.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Individual Needs:
\f1\b0  Some people prefer higher fat for satiety or blood sugar management. The tool can accommodate, as long as it stays within user-defined preferences or at least the AMDR (unless deliberately doing keto). If an athletic user sets a very high carb, fat will by necessity be on the lower end (20% or so); the calculator balances these trade-offs automatically once one sets a macro ratio.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Output:
\f1\b0  e.g. 
\f3\i \'93Fats: 70 g/day (25% of calories)\'94
\f1\i0 . Additional guidance might note the types of fats to emphasize: \'93include sources of omega-3 (e.g. fish, flaxseed) and limit saturated fat to <10% of calories\'94 \'96 perhaps with a footnote if detailed.\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Custom Macro Ratios:
\f1\b0  The tool will likely offer preset ratio options such as \'93Balanced\'94 (e.g. 50%C / 20%P / 30%F), \'93Zone Diet\'94 (40%C / 30%P / 30%F), \'93Low-Carb\'94 (25%C / 30%P / 45%F), \'93High-Protein\'94 (30%C / 30%P / 40%F), etc., in addition to user-defined percentages. Advanced users can manually input any percentages (the interface should ensure they sum to 100%).\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	If the user modifies one macro, the calculator can auto-adjust the others or allow the user to specify each.\
	\'95	It might also allow setting targets in grams instead of percent (some people might say \'93I want 150 g protein\'94 and let the rest auto-balance).\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Micronutrient Recommendations (Optional):
\f1\b0  If the user toggles this feature, the calculator can output a summary of key micronutrient targets based on dietary guidelines:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	For example, it can list RDA or Adequate Intake for vitamins and minerals for the user\'92s demographic (age, sex). e.g. Calcium, Iron, Vitamin D, B12, Sodium, Potassium, etc.\
	\'95	It could highlight ones of special concern:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	If female of reproductive age: ensure adequate iron (18 mg/day) and folate (400 \'b5g/day) are met.\
	\'95	If vegan: emphasize vitamin B\uc0\u8321 \u8322  (supplement ~2.4 \'b5g/day) , iron (~18 mg, from plant sources + vitamin C), calcium (dark greens, fortified plant milks), and maybe iodine or zinc.\
	\'95	If older adult: focus on calcium/vitamin D for bone health, vitamin B\uc0\u8326 /B\u8321 \u8322 , and protein as mentioned.\
	\'95	Athletes: mention electrolytes (e.g. sodium, potassium, magnesium) if heavy sweating, and adequate hydration.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	The micronutrient suggestions would not be individually calculated by default (that requires a full dietary assessment), but the tool can provide general targets and tips. For instance, 
\f3\i \'93Aim for at least 1000 mg calcium daily. If you are dairy-free, include fortified alternatives or leafy greens. Ensure 15 \'b5g (600 IU) of vitamin D \'96 consider supplements if sunlight exposure is low.\'94
\f1\i0 \
	\'95	If the tool is being very comprehensive, it might allow the user to input a micronutrient focus (say they want to ensure 30 g fiber, or monitor sodium intake, etc.) and incorporate that into the plan output.\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
