\f0\b\fs34 \cf2 Customization for Dietary Preferences and Restrictions
\f1\b0\fs28 \
\
One of the strengths of this calculator is adaptability. After computing basic calorie and macro targets, it adjusts recommendations to align with the user\'92s dietary choices and needs:\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Diet Pattern Modifications:
\f1\b0  The macro distribution and qualitative advice change based on selected diet:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Vegan/Vegetarian:
\f1\b0  Protein will be allocated with plant sources in mind. The tool might suggest slightly higher protein grams to compensate for lower bioavailability of plant protein. It will also encourage combining different plant proteins to get all essential amino acids (e.g. grains + legumes). Key micronutrient advisories: vitamin B\uc0\u8321 \u8322  (since it\'92s not present in unfortified plant foods, supplementation is recommended ), iron (plant iron is non-heme; aim for plenty of beans, lentils, spinach + vitamin C sources to aid absorption), calcium (from fortified plant milks, tofu, leafy greens), omega-3 (ALA sources like flax, chia, walnuts or algal DHA supplements). The calculator could flag these in a \'93Nutrient Notes\'94 section for vegans. For vegetarians, B\u8321 \u8322  is less an issue if dairy/eggs included, but iron can still be worth noting.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Keto:
\f1\b0  The macro output is tailored to keep carbohydrates extremely low (often <50g). The tool will likely prompt the user to focus on high-fat foods: e.g. olive oil, avocado, nuts, seeds, fatty fish, etc., and moderate protein (too much protein can convert to glucose via gluconeogenesis and interfere with ketosis). It might list the carb target in net carbs (carbs minus fiber) since keto dieters track net carbs. A keto mode might also add a caution about the diet being very restrictive and not suitable for everyone (with perhaps a reference to ensure users have informed consent). The micronutrient output could note potential need for electrolytes (sodium, potassium, magnesium) as people on keto often need to consciously get enough of these, and maybe fiber supplements if vegetable intake is insufficient.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Low-Carb (Non-Keto):
\f1\b0  The calculator reduces carb percentage and increases fat and protein accordingly. For example, a user choosing \'93low-carb\'94 might get a ratio like 25% carbs, 30% protein, 45% fat. The output will encourage non-starchy vegetables, and possibly higher fiber from sources like flax/chia (since grains might be limited). It will also emphasize protein and fat sources that are filling and nutritious to make up for reduced carbs.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Paleo:
\f1\b0  As it\'92s more about food choices, the macro ratio might default to moderate carbs (somewhere in 20\'9635% range), high protein (25\'9630%), and the remainder fat (~40% or so) which is in line with what some paleo practitioners end up consuming  . The output will list allowed vs. non-allowed food categories rather than a strict macro rule. For example, it may say 
\f3\i \'93Emphasize lean meats, fish, fruits, veggies, nuts, and seeds. Avoid grains, legumes, dairy, refined sugar, and processed foods.\'94
\f1\i0  Micronutrient notes: Paleo diets often inherently provide high fiber and micronutrients if done with a variety of produce; however, calcium might be lower if no dairy, so alternative calcium sources (bone-in fish, leafy greens) could be recommended.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Other Diets:
\f1\b0  The framework can be expanded. For instance:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f3\i Mediterranean:
\f1\i0  The calculator could adjust to a higher fat (mostly unsaturated) and moderate carb/protein distribution, and encourage foods like olive oil, fish, whole grains, legumes, veggies, and moderate wine.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Zone Diet:
\f1\i0  Fixed 40/30/30 ratio \'96 the user could simply input that or select it from presets.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Gluten-Free:
\f1\i0  If a user marks this, it doesn\'92t change macro targets but the advice on carb sources will focus on rice, quinoa, potatoes, etc., instead of wheat.\
	\'95	
\f3\i Intermittent Fasting:
\f1\i0  While IF is more about meal timing than macros, the tool could accommodate by structuring the output (like advising two larger meals instead of three, etc.) \'96 but that might be beyond scope for calculation.\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	The key is that the macro calculator\'92s outputs are 
\f3\i contextualized
\f1\i0  by the diet choice: it\'92s not just numbers, but also source recommendations and any necessary warnings (e.g. \'93with your very low carb intake, ensure you\'92re getting enough fiber and minerals \'96 consider a multivitamin or consult a professional\'94).\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Allergies/Intolerances:
\f1\b0  The output will avoid recommending foods that the user cannot have:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	If someone indicates 
\f2\b nut allergy
\f1\b0 , any generic advice about \'93nuts and seeds for healthy fats\'94 would be replaced with alternatives (olive oil, avocado, etc.). The micronutrient section might note other ways to get nutrients one might get from nuts (e.g. magnesium from greens or seeds if those are okay, omega-3 from fish or flax, etc.).\
	\'95	
\f2\b Lactose intolerance or dairy-free:
\f1\b0  The tool would suggest non-dairy sources of protein and calcium (fortified plant milks, tofu, leafy greens, almonds if not allergic, etc.). If the user is okay with other animal products, it might emphasize meats, eggs, fish for protein instead of whey or milk.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Gluten allergy (Celiac):
\f1\b0  Carb suggestions would highlight gluten-free options (rice, oats if certified GF, quinoa, potatoes, corn, etc.). Fiber via fruits, veg, and GF whole grains will be stressed.\
	\'95	
\f2\b Other:
\f1\b0  The user could input any exclusion (shellfish, soy, etc.). The logic is that any dietary advice or sample foods in the output filter those out. This is more about how the info is presented rather than the numeric calculations. If the calculator has a recipe or meal suggestion component, it would likewise exclude those items in generating sample menus.\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Goal-based Macro Tweaks:
\f1\b0  Depending on the goal (loss/gain), the macro breakdown might be adjusted:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	For 
\f2\b Weight Loss:
\f1\b0  Often slightly higher protein (to preserve muscle and promote satiety) and slightly lower carbs or fats (to achieve the calorie deficit). The calculator could, for instance, bump protein to the higher end of the allowed range, and reduce either carbs or fat based on user preference. If no preference, it might reduce carbs moderately as many find that helpful for appetite control (unless the person is an endurance athlete who needs carbs). It might also emphasize high-fiber, high-volume foods to help the user feel full in a deficit.\
	\'95	For 
\f2\b Muscle Gain:
\f1\b0  Protein is kept high (though beyond a point extra protein doesn\'92t increase muscle, but it\'92s often kept around ~2 g/kg). Carbs are important as they are protein-sparing and fuel heavy training \'96 so a slight tilt toward more carbs might be given if the user\'92s goal is muscle gain and they indicated regular strength training. Fats can be kept moderate. Essentially the extra calories in a surplus could be split between carbs and fats per user comfort, but many muscle-building diets go high on carbs to drive insulin and anabolic environment, unless the person prefers otherwise.\
	\'95	For 
\f2\b Recomposition (lose fat + gain muscle):
\f1\b0  If a user somehow indicates this dual goal (which usually means very slow body change), the calculator might recommend a small deficit or at maintenance with high protein and strength training. This scenario might come up if someone selects both fat loss and muscle gain as goals \'96 the tool could then suggest a conservative approach (slight deficit or maintenance + heavy training).\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Manual Overrides & Advanced Adjustments:
\f1\b0  Throughout the macro planning, the user can manually tweak values:\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	Suppose the calculator suggests 130 g protein but the user prefers 150 g \'96 they should be able to input that, and then the calculator will reallocate calories from carbs or fats to account for the extra 20 g protein (which is ~80 kcal). The interface might have sliders or input boxes for grams or percents of each macro.\
	\'95	The tool could also allow the user to pick which macro to prioritize when adjusting. For example, an \'93advanced mode\'94 toggle could open up fields like:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	\'93Set protein to ___ g\'94 (or as %).\
	\'95	\'93Set fat to at least ___ g or ___%\'94 (maybe they want a minimum fat for satiety or hormone reasons).\
	\'95	\'93Carbs will then fill the remainder of calories\'94 (by default).\
\pard\tqr\tx500\tx660\li660\fi-660\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Example:
\f1\b0  An advanced user might say \'93I want 40% carbs, 30% protein, 30% fat exactly\'94 \'96 they input those numbers and the calculator uses them (and perhaps validates the totals 100%). Another might say \'93I know I need 120 g protein and I never go below 50 g fat for health, let the rest be carbs\'94 \'96 the tool would take those as constraints and compute that maybe that ends up being ~55% carbs given their calories.\
	\'95	Additionally, advanced users could override 
\f2\b which equation
\f1\b0  is used for RMR (maybe to compare results). The specification would include that as an option: e.g. a dropdown to select Mifflin vs Cunningham vs custom value. Similarly, they might override the PAL and directly input a custom daily calorie burn number if they have one from a fitness tracker.\
\pard\tqr\tx100\tx260\li260\fi-260\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	
\f2\b Algorithm/Logic Flow Summary:
\f1\b0  Bringing it together, the internal logic follows a sequence:\
\pard\tqr\tx660\tx820\li820\fi-820\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf2 	1.	
\f2\b Input Phase:
\f1\b0  Collect all user inputs (with defaults). Validate them (e.g. ensure height/weight are in plausible range, percent macros sum to 100 if custom, etc.).\

\f4 	2.	
\f2\b RMR Calculation:
\f1\b0  If custom RMR given, use it; else compute via chosen equation (default Mifflin-St Jeor). If using a lean-mass formula and body fat% is missing, fall back to Mifflin or prompt for needed data.\

\f4 	3.	
\f2\b Base TDEE:
\f1\b0  Multiply RMR by PAL (or add exercise calories) to get an initial daily expenditure. This is the 
\f2\b unadjusted TDEE
\f1\b0 .\

\f4 	4.	
\f2\b Add TEF:
\f1\b0  Either by multiplying by ~1.1 or using a more detailed method if applicable. Obtain 
\f2\b maintenance TDEE
\f1\b0 .\

\f4 	5.	
\f2\b Goal Adjustment:
\f1\b0  Modify the calorie target up or down according to goal. This finalizes the 
\f2\b daily calorie target
\f1\b0 .\

\f4 	6.	
\f2\b Macronutrient Allocation:
\f1\b0  Assign protein, fat, carbs based on either defaults or user-specified ratios:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	Determine protein grams (based on g/kg guidelines or percent). Calories from protein = grams \'d7 4.\
	\'95	Determine fat grams (based on % or g/kg min). Calories from fat = grams \'d7 9.\
	\'95	Remaining calories go to carbs. Convert to grams (\'f74).\
	\'95	If user set all three explicitly (or via a preset), just calculate grams from percentages of the target calories.\
	\'95	Ensure none of the macros violate general health thresholds (if so, possibly flag a warning rather than hard override, since the user might be intentionally doing so for a diet like keto).\
\pard\tqr\tx660\tx820\li820\fi-820\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf2 	7.	
\f2\b Micronutrient & Additional Recommendations:
\f1\b0  Based on the profile and diet, prepare any micronutrient targets or warnings. Also possibly generate a short advice section (like hydration needs, or reminder about exercise for weight goals, etc., though that might be beyond the core \'93calculator\'94 spec).\

\f4 	8.	
\f2\b Output Generation:
\f1\b0  Present the results in a clear format:\
\pard\tqr\tx900\tx1060\li1060\fi-1060\sl324\slmult1\sb240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\'95	Daily Calorie target (TDEE or adjusted) with explanation.\
	\'95	Macronutrient targets (grams & %), often best in a small table or well-formatted list.\
	\'95	If enabled, a list of micronutrient recommendations or any notes specific to the user\'92s inputs (diet, allergies, etc.).\
	\'95	If applicable, a few 
\f3\i tips or options
\f1\i0  (like \'93to achieve this protein intake, you could eat X, Y, Z\'85\'94 but that may be optional).\
